       <p>AI tools are no longer experimental side projects in design teams. In 2026, the agencies shipping fastest treat Claude, ChatGPT, Figma AI, and Cursor as part of the delivery stack, with clear guardrails so people still own the final call.</p>
 <h2>Where AI saves the most time</h2>
 <ul><li>Research synthesis, turning interview notes, support tickets, and analytics into structured problem statements.</li><li>First-draft flows, generating wireframe variants to react to, not to ship blindly.</li><li>Copy exploration, headline and microcopy options grounded in brand voice guidelines.</li><li>Design QA · spotting spacing inconsistencies, contrast issues, and component drift across screens.</li><li>Handoff prep, annotating flows and edge cases before dev kickoff.</li></ul>
+<p>Notice what is not on that list: strategy, prioritization, and anything a client signs off on. The pattern behind every item is the same, AI is generating volume for a human to react to, or checking consistency against rules a human already set. The moment a task requires deciding what the product should do rather than producing variants of a decision already made, it moves off this list.</p>
 <h2>What still needs a senior designer</h2>
 <p>AI accelerates drafts; it does not replace judgment. Information hierarchy, brand differentiation, accessibility tradeoffs, and stakeholder alignment still require experienced designers who understand business context. We use AI to remove repetitive work so designers spend more time on decisions that move metrics.</p>
+<p>The failure mode worth naming is plausible-but-wrong output. AI-generated flows look finished, which makes their errors more expensive than an obviously rough sketch: a missing error state or a permissions edge case ships because the artifact looked complete. This is why our review gates are placed exactly where AI output enters the pipeline, not at the end of it. A senior designer reviewing an AI draft in ten minutes catches what a junior accepting it silently would ship.</p>
 <h2>Our practical workflow</h2>
 <p>Discovery stays human-first: workshops, user interviews, and written briefs. During design, we generate alternatives with AI, critique them as a team, and merge the best ideas into Figma. Every screen passes a human review checklist before development. During build, engineers use Copilot and Cursor for boilerplate, never for unchecked business logic.</p>
 <h2>Measuring whether it works</h2>
-<p>Track cycle time from brief to approved prototype, revision rounds per milestone, and defect rate post-handoff. Teams doing this well typically cut discovery-to-design time by 20-35% without increasing rework, because AI handles volume, and humans handle taste.</p>
+<p>Track cycle time from brief to approved prototype, revision rounds per milestone, and defect rate post-handoff. In our own delivery tracking, the compression shows up between brief and first reviewable prototype, the volume-heavy stage, while review and alignment time stays roughly flat, because AI handles volume, and humans handle taste. If cycle time drops but revision rounds climb, the AI is producing drafts faster than it is producing good drafts, and the process needs a tighter gate, not more generation.</p>
+<h2>What clients should ask any AI-assisted studio</h2>
+<p>Three questions separate a working AI process from a marketing line. Which stages use AI and which never do, a real answer names specific stages and specific tools. Who reviews AI output before it reaches me, the answer should be a person with a name and seniority, not "our process." And does AI-assisted mean cheaper, faster, or better, an honest studio picks at most two and can show where the third was traded. How we run this on live projects is documented in <a href="/services/ai-assisted-development">AI-assisted development</a>, including the human gates on every send.</p>
