-      <p>Founders often ask whether to launch on Bubble, Webflow, or Framer, or invest in custom Next.js from day one. The honest answer depends on validation stage, team capacity, and what you need to learn in the next 90 days.</p>
+      <p>Founders often ask whether to launch on Bubble, Webflow, or Framer, or invest in custom Next.js from day one. The honest answer depends on validation stage, team capacity, and what you need to learn in the next 90 days. The wrong answer is choosing by ideology, "real products need real code" and "code is dead" both cost founders quarters they did not have.</p>
 <h2>When no-code is the right call</h2>
-<ul><li>You need to test demand with real payments or bookings in weeks, not months.</li><li>The core workflow is standard (marketplace, directory, landing + CRM handoff).</li><li>You expect significant pivots before committing to a engineering team.</li><li>Integrations are limited to well-supported Zapier/Make connectors.</li></ul>
+<ul><li>You need to test demand with real payments or bookings in weeks, not months.</li><li>The core workflow is standard (marketplace, directory, landing + CRM handoff).</li><li>You expect significant pivots before committing to an engineering team.</li><li>Integrations are limited to well-supported Zapier/Make connectors.</li></ul>
+<p>What each platform is actually for matters more than brand preference. Webflow and Framer are publishing tools: marketing sites, content, landing pages with forms. Bubble is an application builder: logged-in users, workflows, data you query. Glide and Softr wrap spreadsheets into internal tools. Choosing Bubble for a content site or Webflow for an app is the most common self-inflicted no-code failure we see, the platform gets blamed for a casting error.</p>
 <h2>When custom code makes sense early</h2>
 <ul><li>Proprietary logic, complex permissions, or real-time collaboration are core to the product.</li><li>You already have engineering hires and need a codebase they can extend.</li><li>Performance, security, or compliance requirements exceed no-code platform limits.</li><li>Investors or enterprise buyers expect audit trails, SLAs, or self-hosted options.</li></ul>
+<p>There is also a cost-shape difference founders underestimate. No-code front-loads speed and defers cost: platform fees scale with usage, workarounds accumulate, and the rebuild, if the product works, is a real project. Custom code front-loads cost and defers speed: slower first launch, but each following release gets cheaper on a codebase your team owns. Neither is free; you are choosing when to pay.</p>
 <h2>The hybrid path we use often</h2>
-<p>Many clients start no-code for validation, then rebuild the validated slice in custom code while keeping marketing sites on Webflow or Framer. A local services marketplace we shipped went live in six weeks on no-code, then migrated checkout and provider matching to a custom backend once unit economics were proven.</p>
+<p>Many clients start no-code for validation, then rebuild the validated slice in custom code while keeping marketing sites on Webflow or Framer. <a href="/work/marketplace-mvp">LocalServe</a>, a local services marketplace we shipped, went live in six weeks on no-code, then migrated checkout and provider matching to a custom backend once unit economics were proven. The marketing site never moved, there was no reason for it to.</p>
+<p>The signals that it is time to switch are operational, not emotional: you are paying for workarounds more than features, a core workflow has hit a hard platform limit, support tickets trace to platform behavior you cannot change, or an enterprise deal is blocked on compliance the platform cannot offer. Migrate the slice that hit the wall, not the whole product. Rebuilding everything at once converts a working business into a six-month engineering project.</p>
 <h2>Decision framework</h2>
 <p>Ask: What must be true in 90 days? If the answer is 'learn whether anyone pays,' bias no-code. If the answer is 'prove we can operate at scale,' bias custom. Either way, design the UX properly first, switching platforms is expensive, but switching bad UX is worse.</p>
+<p>Three more questions worth answering honestly before choosing. Who maintains this in month four, if the answer is "nobody technical," a no-code tool your ops team can edit beats a codebase that waits for a hire. What is the riskiest assumption, build only the slice that tests it. And what does a dead end cost, if a rebuild later would sink you, spend more now; if it is two months of work, validation speed wins. We build <a href="/services/no-code-low-code">both paths</a>, which is exactly why we have no platform to sell you.</p>
