 <p>Define a single outcome users should achieve in their first session. For B2B SaaS, this might be 'publish first report', 'invite first teammate', or 'run first payroll draft'. Scope everything else around helping users reach that moment reliably.</p>
 <h2>MVP scope framework we use</h2>
 <ul><li>Core workflow: the smallest end-to-end flow that delivers value.</li><li>Critical supporting flows: authentication, permissions, and basic settings.</li><li>Operational minimum: error handling, admin visibility, and support paths.</li><li>Not now list: features that sound useful but do not improve time-to-value.</li></ul>
+<h2>What is actually different about a UK launch</h2>
+<p>Most MVP advice is geography-neutral; a few things about the UK are not, and they are cheap to get right at MVP stage and expensive to retrofit.</p>
+<ul><li>Data protection: UK GDPR applies from user one, and most companies processing personal data owe the ICO a data protection fee and registration. Decide at design time where personal data lives and how a user exports or deletes it, this is a schema decision, not a policy page.</li><li>Billing: UK B2B buyers expect prices in GBP and, for recurring billing, Direct Debit is a first-class rail, not an afterthought, many UK finance teams prefer it to cards. Card-only checkout is a quiet conversion tax on UK enterprise deals.</li><li>Investor mechanics: UK angels commonly invest through SEIS/EIS and will ask early whether you have HMRC advance assurance. It costs little to arrange and signals you have done the homework.</li><li>Enterprise and public sector: UK procurement frequently asks for Cyber Essentials certification, and selling to government runs through frameworks like G-Cloud. An MVP does not need either, but knowing they exist shapes what "enterprise-ready" means on your roadmap.</li><li>Company mechanics: Companies House formation is fast and cheap, so incorporation is rarely the bottleneck founders fear, the operational work is the registrations that follow it.</li></ul>
 <h2>Design before development, always</h2>
 <p>Even under tight timelines, UX architecture and interactive prototypes reduce expensive rework. Teams that skip this step usually pay for it later with rushed redesigns, inconsistent onboarding, and engineering slowdowns.</p>
 <h2>Launch with analytics from day one</h2>
-<p>Track activation, time-to-value, and feature adoption from the first release. UK investors and early enterprise buyers care less about feature count and more about evidence that users reach value consistently.</p>
+<p>Track activation, time-to-value, and feature adoption from the first release. UK investors and early enterprise buyers care less about feature count and more about evidence that users reach value consistently. In practice that means three instrumented numbers you can show in a meeting: what percentage of signups reach the core outcome, how long it takes them, and whether they come back in week two. A founder who knows those three numbers runs a different fundraise from one with a feature list.</p>
 <h2>A practical 12-week MVP plan</h2>
 <ul><li>Weeks 1-2: discovery, ICP alignment, and UX flows.</li><li>Weeks 3-5: high-fidelity UI and clickable prototype validation.</li><li>Weeks 6-10: build core flows, QA, and event instrumentation.</li><li>Weeks 11-12: launch, support, and first iteration sprint.</li></ul>
+<p>Two UK-specific notes on the plan. Put the data-protection decisions, what personal data you store, where, and how it leaves, into weeks 1-2 with the UX flows, because retrofitting them after build is rework. And if SEIS/EIS investment is on your path, start advance assurance in parallel with the build; HMRC's timeline is not yours to control. The plan itself is the same discipline we apply on <a href="/solutions/saas">SaaS engagements</a>: one core outcome, instrumented from day one, shipped in phases the team can absorb.</p>
     </div>
