-      <p>Most SaaS activation problems are onboarding problems. Users sign up with intent, hit friction in the first session, and never return. These five patterns show up repeatedly in projects where activation improved measurably.</p>
+      <p>Most SaaS activation problems are onboarding problems. Users sign up with intent, hit friction in the first session, and never return. The expensive part is that the team usually finds out weeks later, in a churn report, long after the moment the user gave up. These five patterns show up repeatedly in projects where activation improved measurably.</p>
 <h2>1. Progressive disclosure beats feature tours</h2>
 <p>Long product tours feel comprehensive and perform terribly. Show users one job-to-be-done path first, import data, invite a teammate, or complete one core action, then unlock advanced features after success.</p>
+<p>The reason tours fail is that they explain the product instead of operating it. A user who has just signed up has one job in mind; every tooltip that is not about that job is a delay. Progressive disclosure inverts the tour: the interface starts with the minimum surface for the first job and grows as the user succeeds. The practical rule we apply: nothing appears in the first session unless it is needed to finish the first job or leads directly to the second one.</p>
 <h2>2. Role-based entry points</h2>
 <p>Admin, manager, and end-user flows should not share the same empty dashboard. Ask role upfront and land each persona on a relevant home screen with one clear next step.</p>
+<p>Role matters because the definition of "activated" differs by persona. An admin is activated when the workspace is configured and colleagues are invited. An end user is activated when they finish one real task inside the product. If both land on the same screen, at least one of them is looking at the wrong product. On <a href="/work/payroll-pro-saas">PayrollPro</a>, separating the admin setup path from the employee first-run path was the single largest structural change of the engagement, most "activation" complaints turned out to be employees stranded on screens built for admins.</p>
 <h2>3. Checklist with real value at each step</h2>
 <p>Checklists work when each item delivers immediate payoff, not when they exist to gamify setup. Tie every step to an outcome the user can see: a report populated, a notification sent, a workflow running.</p>
+<p>A useful test for every checklist item: if the user skipped it, would anything in the product be worse for them today? "Complete your profile" usually fails that test. "Connect your accounting system", after which their real numbers appear on the dashboard, passes it. Order items by payoff, not by your system's setup sequence, and let users finish out of order. The checklist is a value tour, not a compliance form.</p>
 <h2>4. SSO and permissions clarity</h2>
 <p>After SSO rollouts, activation often drops because users land in the wrong workspace or lack permissions. Surface workspace context, explain missing access, and route to an admin, don't dead-end with generic errors.</p>
+<p>The failure pattern is consistent: SSO ships, login friction drops, and activation falls anyway, because authentication was never the real barrier. The barrier is what happens two screens later, when a user can log in but cannot do anything. Every permission error should answer three questions in plain language: what you tried to reach, why you cannot, and who can fix it, with a one-click request to that person. "Contact your administrator" with no route to the administrator is a dead end wearing a helpful tone.</p>
 <h2>5. Time-to-value metrics on the dashboard</h2>
-<p>Show progress toward the user's goal inside the product. A payroll client improved activation after adding a simple 'setup health' panel with three milestones instead of a static welcome modal.</p>
+<p>Show progress toward the user's goal inside the product. On <a href="/work/payroll-pro-saas">PayrollPro</a>, a UK/EU payroll SaaS, a simple 'setup health' panel with three milestones replaced a static welcome modal, so every login showed the distance to a working payroll run instead of a greeting.</p>
+<p>Pick the one metric that means the product is doing its job, first payroll run completed, first invoice sent, first report shared, and make progress toward it visible from day one. Time-to-value is also an internal instrument: instrument each onboarding step, watch where users stall, and fix the step, not the messaging around it. Teams that measure time-to-first-value weekly ship different roadmaps from teams that measure signups.</p>
+<h2>Where to start</h2>
+<p>Do not redesign onboarding in one release. Instrument the current funnel first, find the single step where the most users stall, and apply whichever pattern above addresses it. Onboarding rebuilt in phases beside a live product is the same discipline as any workflow change: one measured step at a time. That is the approach we take on <a href="/solutions/saas">SaaS engagements</a> generally, and it is why most onboarding work starts as a two-week diagnostic rather than a redesign.</p>
     </div>
